The `ask` command is extremely powerful. Use it to utilize a model for effecient one-shot solutions, especially those that need knowledge of many files at once. Entire directory trees (-p) can be included with the prompt and multiple (-p) can be used at once. Avoid using single-file (-f) and markdown extraction (-e) for for now because I can never get it right. Utilize the -1 model for smaller prompts and fastest results, the -2 model (default) for most things, and -3 when we really need a smart reply on lots of data.
#For example, to ask for a new command in the style of all existing commands and extract the python result from the default model I would use: `ask -p /bin -e python bin/new_command.py "Write new_command which provides a summary of all the existing commands"`.

Remember that `ask` uses general models with no specific knowledge of our project. To give it context, use the `-p` flag. Before using `ask -p`, it's a good workflow to first run `tree` on the same directory to see exactly which files will be included. `tree` is an accurate preview because both it and `ask` honor `.skignore` files, unlike `ls`.

Don't do too much at once. Only ONE markdown code block will be auto-saved (```) or ONE command executed per interactive chat round (don't do both at once). Don't put anything after a command or markdown block (wait for the next round to do more).

